    tier: T2
    action: added
    distills: "The rulebook clause the flow-list design run escalated to W11 by name, answered. wrap-not-truncate keeps its predicate and its Tier-1 red; a design may record violated and ship the cut only where a declared Frame constraint makes wrapping impossible, the full value stays reachable by a named route, and the cut is visible. Four alternatives recorded as rejected, including weakening the predicate (it cannot see row height, so it would have to trust a data- attribute the design writes about itself) and a seventh verdict violated-by-constraint (reads as absolution). Synthesized UP from the run's collision table, which is archived out of git at the epic close - the argument had to move to a permanent tier or die with the run."
  - path: docs/decisions/decisions-index.html
    tier: T2
    action: modified
    distills: "The DEC-34 row, so the record is reachable index-first per the documentation system's read-index-first rule. Its rejected-alternatives summary carries the review's correction with the record it summarises: an added receipt verdict is the eighth beside DEC-15's fixed seven, not the seventh."
  - path: docs/design-rules/design-rules-index.html
    tier: T2
    action: modified
    distills: "Two amendments the epic deferred to its close. (1) wrap-not-truncate carries DEC-34's escape clause and points at it; its Tier-1 check text now states the escape is declared on the receipt, never detected - the predicate is unchanged. (2) The chromatic-meanings table gains what DEC-30 argued and W2 deliberately held back so the four screen runs would pin an unchanged substrate: StatusDot as a second owner of ok (a connection proven live) and warn (a connection that is not capturing), and ink-mute as a first-class row - the not-yet-known channel, a lifecycle state and never a verdict. The rulebook blob moves aeff5b0 -> 849cc8e; all four design runs are archived and so exempt from the pin check."
